## 2. Architecture and Design Principles

Follow these principles:

1. Non-blocking, bounded data-plane processing.
2. No userspace packet queuing or packet forwarding dependency.
3. Fixed-size packet sampling windows.
4. Kernel-based flow tracking and efficient userspace communication.
5. Integer-only production inference.
6. Bounded memory and CPU consumption.
7. IPv4/IPv6 dual-stack compatibility.
8. Fail-open networking if classification fails.
9. Exact Python/C feature parity.
10. Production-grade error handling, resource cleanup and observability.

Do not assume that an architecture is supported simply because it works on a desktop Linux distribution.

Before implementing it, identify the target Linux kernel version, available BPF features, RDK-B version, Yocto release, target architecture and required libraries.

Where platform details are unavailable, establish an explicit reference configuration and distinguish verified functionality from platform-dependent assumptions.

